  "body": "Currently `ASCII upper hex digit` and `ASCII lower hex digit` are defined as:\r\n> An ASCII upper hex digit is an ASCII digit or a code point in the range U+0041 (A) to U+0046 (F), inclusive.\r\n> An ASCII lower hex digit is an ASCII digit or a code point in the range U+0061 (a) to U+0066 (f), inclusive.\r\n\r\nThese definitions render [hexademical character consumption in parser](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#hexademical-character-reference-state) incorrect:\r\nconsumption algorithm should distinguish `ASCII upper hex digit`, `ASCII lower hex digit` and `ASCII digit`.\r\n\r\n However, with current definition `ASCII digit` is a subset of two first. Moreover, it's not quite semantically correct that `lower hex digit` or `upper hex digit` include `ASCII digit`. \r\n\r\nI can issue a PR, although I'm not sure about other usages of terms in specs. It would be nice if someone competent  review all these usages and determine which definition is implied in each case.",
